A||||16||88formula||Centerforce or Ram clutch vote||||9||
Z||000000||88formula||02-21-2000||05:51 PM||||I'm trying to make up my mind as to which clutch I should use in my Formula. The Centerforce clutch according to the search I did on this forum has a lighter clutch pedal and a tenacious grip for engines with lots of power, but it costs more ($353+T.O bearing $52=$405 for dual friction and $328 for Centerforce 1). The Ram clutch also seems to be a good clutch that costs less but with a heavier pedal ($272 with T.O bearing). So I'm going to leave it to you guys because I cant make up my mind. Would you get the Centerforce dual friction (1), Cenerforce one (2), or the Ram racing clutch (3)? I am going to put a Comp-u-cam cam in, port the heads and exhaust manifolds, a Borla cat back exhaust, Power pulley, K+N filter, Accel super coil, and gasket match the intake manifold. What is your vote? 1,2, or 3.||||reg||9
Z||000001||william_thorniii||02-21-2000||06:34 PM||||88,<P>I hate to complicate things, but usually do.  I have not heard many good things about either set-up.  Although for relatively stock applications I hear that the RAM works fine.  However, if you intend on hot-rodding this car in the future, you should look up the Fiero Shop out in CA.  George is having a kevlar clutch made for his shop specifically for the Fiero that is supposedly top notch.  Some people that I trust deeply have sworn to the quality of this clutch maker (local cluth shop).  Anyway, it can't hurt to add options.  Also I would like to say that IF I were to go with an aftermarket clutch it would be from the Fiero Shop since I have heard such negative things about the others.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||12
Z||000002||Eric||02-21-2000||06:38 PM||||The reason I like the dual friction so much is, it really helps to keep the hydraulic parts in working order.  The light pedal probably puts half the stress on the master/ slave cylinder.  I used to have a considerable amount of trouble with these parts.  I was constantly bleeding the clutch and was pretty much willing to try anything (even thought about ATF, yikes!)  Since I replace all these parts, I want to keep them working for a very long time so I think it's worth the extra money (paid around $370 for clutch and to bearing from Summit).  Plus it's easier on your leg in stop and go traffic.<P>People have said the pressure plate would explode, but maybe that's just the experience of a few hard core drag racers, I don't know.  I do know the clutch is grippier and doesn't slip at any rpm.  I've got about 190 hp from the crank (estimate), the clutch is overkill, but that's okay with me.||||reg||10
Z||000003||88formula||02-21-2000||06:55 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.fieroshop.net/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroshop.net/</A> Thanks<p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 02-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||william_thorniii||02-21-2000||07:09 PM||||Found the message from the list in my folders.  Here goes:<P>Date:  Thu, 17 Feb 00 13:18PM EST  <BR>From:  "Michael W. Smith" &lt;mrwax@macrat.com&gt;    Add To Address Book  Add To Junk Mail Blocker List  <BR>To:  RCAVI8TER@aol.co, fiero-list@fiero.org  <BR>Subject:  re: (Fiero) Clutches  <BR> <BR>   <BR>More Details  <BR>   <BR>--------------------------------------------------------------------------------<BR> <BR> <BR>    <P>&gt;&gt;I need a clutch that can handle the<BR>power. I really do not want to use the CenterForce clutch but if it is<BR>the<BR>best out there I just might have to.  By the way I plan on doing<BR>extremely<BR>hard lauches (Drag Racing) so take this into account.<BR>Chris Puckett<BR>&gt;&gt;<P>I have had nothing but grief with the Centerforce Dual Friction. I just<BR>ordered mine from Centerforce direct. it's the part number on their<BR>site. Apparently there's supposed to be one Archie had made special, but<BR>people write back to me and say it is not grabbing well either. I have a<BR>225 hp DOHC, and it slips, and was a nightmare at the drags. Just<BR>embarassing.<P>I've installed about 8-10 clutches on V-6s and this is the WORST<BR>clutch--but just as bad as the FOCOA "big clutch"--I've tried. Stock LUK<BR>clutches are better, in my opinion. Rams are fun, but break into peices.<P>Call the Fiero Shop in Fremont, Cal. He is having a local clutch maker I<BR>really like, make an All-Kevlar clutch with seriously increased clamping<BR>force, more like 2100 lbs rather than the CFs wimpy 1200 or so. It's in<BR>the $350 range (similar price to CF) . I have installed one on a pals<BR>car and could burn them off during a test run.  My CF has YET to burn<BR>them off, with more than 10K miles on it.<P><BR>(510) 732-9516  ask for George.<P>-MWS<P>-- <BR>All messages to the Fiero-list must be strictly Fiero-related.  Do not respond<BR>to off-topic mail. Fiero-list rules & information: <A HREF="http://www-2.fiero.org/list" TARGET=_blank>http://www-2.fiero.org/list</A> <BR>To unsubscribe from the Fiero-list, email fiero-list-request@fiero.org with<BR>Subject: unsubscribe, or visit  <A HREF="http://www-2.fiero.org/list" TARGET=_blank>http://www-2.fiero.org/list</A> <BR> <BR> <BR>||||reg||10
Z||000005||fieroshop||03-21-2000||01:21 PM||||Some of you guys are thinking that a dual friction clutch is the best. Unfortunatly that is not true. My guess is some of you don't know what a dual friction is. It is nothing more that having one type of friction materials on one side and a different material on the other side. This doae not make it a better cltch. The only time it is a good clutch is for specific use. Like in racing where you have an extreme abuse then you would go to a friction on one side and metal on the side. Again this causes problems because now you have one side that will eat either the flywheel or the pressure plate. That is why we have gone to an all kevlar disc that give s you a double your previous cltch miles. I know you must say that that is unheard of in this industry but believe me it is all true.<BR>Eric, the dual friction material does nothing for your hydraulics. Some body is pulling your leg.<p>[This message has been edited by fieroshop (edited 03-21-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000006||Alex4mula||03-21-2000||03:03 PM||||I had use a Centerforce DF in my 88 Formula with good results. I have a Compucam, K&N, Jacobs ignition, no cat and HTech chip. Grip was good and it was smooth to operate. But it didn't last as long as I wanted. I will need one soon & was looking for another CF. But I would trade for a Kevlar if it really last longer and does not chatter during engagement. I just hate that. Does this Kevlar one does this? Any users out there other than the supplier?||||reg||1
Z||000007||1FST2M6||03-21-2000||03:27 PM||||i had BOTH the RAM and the the DF centerforce. under any hard launch (with or with out my nitrous) they would both slip. if your in it for a drag racing or road racing (SCCA, Solo or IT) I'd go with somthing else. check into the Kevlar unit from the FieroShop. I have an ACT (Advanced Clutch Technology) extreme pressure plate and 4 puck unsprung disc. I put out with the nitrous on at 1300rpm 310lbft at the flywheel and I have never had my clutch slip. IT chatters a bit in traffic but not so much that it's undrivable. i set up BlackGt (Todd) with a Borg/Beck basicly stock pressure plate and the same disc. He seems happy with it. and he drives in traffic about 2 hours a day to and from work. Hey todd you paying attention...||||reg||1
Z||000008||mwbackus||03-21-2000||03:32 PM||||The RAM racing clutch from the Fiero Store uses a Kevlar Compound disc.  They work just fine and will probably out last the car. It does use a heavy duty pressure plate and will increase clutch pedal pressure.<P>The "Centerforce II" is also a good option.  Even though the model used for the Fiero does not incorporate the counter weighted presure plate due to size constraints, they do state that an alternate method has been employed to achieve the same functionality.  The "Centerforce I" is not a choice I would consider. <P>IMO, either clutch is overkill for a 140hp V6.  I choose to run the Fiero Store's RAM Kevlar system based on the increased longevity of the "internal" components rather than any appreciable effect on performance.  I really couldnt care less about increased pedal pressure...its a "sports car".||||reg||1
Z||000009||fieroshop||03-21-2000||03:53 PM||||MWBACKUS, the ram clutch is a composition kevlar clutch and that means that it has about 10% of kevlar in it. Our clutch is 100% kevlar with a warranty that no other clutch manufacturer Has. We are proud of our clutch and are selling more and more everyday and will continue to do so . Our clutches have been on the market for a year now with no problems reported. Some of these clutches have over 15000 miles of use here in the Bay Area with all our hills and reports are extremely good.||||reg||1
Z||000010||JIM GOOSE||03-21-2000||04:03 PM|||| I have the CF dual friction which originally bought due to the fact that it seemed like the best and most all mags raved how good it was. I have a twin-turbo kit which I plan to install when get around to it. My car with mods is on <A HREF="http://www.hamptonmotorsports.com." TARGET=_blank>www.hamptonmotorsports.com.</A>   So far havent had any problems and it has light pedal pressure & bites good, have about 6k miles on it so far.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Phaeton||03-22-2000||01:09 AM||||My sister says kevlar is bulletproof, and she's a cop.<BR>So I just ordered the Fieroshop clutch and will mention how it works later this summer.<BR>I babied the last factory clutch to almost twenty thousand miles when it started slipping, just after I had teased a camaro up to 105, he was struggling and I floored it! The revs jumped and the Fiero didn't, dang but I sincerely hate that!||||reg||12
Z||000012||mwbackus||03-22-2000||03:06 AM||||FIEROSHOP:  Dupont Kevlar is manufactured as a "fiber", therefore all kevlar clutch disc material is correctly referred to as either  a composite or compound.  To state that a clutch disc could contain 100% kevlar would be somewhat misleading.||||reg||12
Z||000013||Phaeton||03-22-2000||03:46 AM||||My 100% cotton t-shirt?  ||||reg||1
Z||000014||fieroshop||03-22-2000||10:55 AM||||mwbackus, not according to my builder. If the clutch disc is not yellow in color then you have a percent of kevlar in the friction material. Kevlar is yellow in color so look for it that way. ||||reg||6
Z||000015||88formula||03-22-2000||05:37 PM||||I thought this thread was dead weeks ago.||||reg||1
Z||000016||BlackGT||03-23-2000||01:06 PM||||Well folks,<BR>It`s been about 2 weeks since the clutch install and I`m still grining! The set up that 1FST2M6 uses is the same with the <BR>exception of a little stronger that stock<BR>Borg&Beck pressure plate and a kevlar 4 puck<BR>disc. The setup works well together and when<BR>ever I jump on it THERE IS NO SLIP! Hear that<BR>Trav...... It does however chatter and shake<BR>the dash during stop and go traffic(Mine is a daily driver) but the lite pedal feel helps<BR>to make that bearable. Once out and away from traffic the engagement is positive and<BR>you will chirp the tires during quick shifting. Overall in my opinion this is a excellent alternative to stock pieces and <BR>according to 1FST2M6 I should not be looking<BR>for a replacement for a long time even with<BR>some modifications like Nitrous and moderate<BR>abuse! We`ll see ............. ||||reg||6
